        // every existing caller still passes 400ms, so this reproduces
        // today's exact constants everywhere unchanged (400*1.5=600,
        // 400*1.75=700). fadeMsOverride decouples the fade from the SHAPE
        // morph's own duration for callers that want the shape-swap fast
        // (matching every other 'hide' trigger) but the fade itself
        // slower — hero-exit's own "keep rotating while it dissolves"
        // request specifically (particle-animation-loop.js): the shape
        // settles at the normal 400ms pace, then lingers, still spinning,
        // through a longer fade instead of everything ending together.
        // hideDelayMs keeps the SAME ratio to whichever fade is in play
        // (1.75/1.5 ≈ 1.1667x it), so the render loop still only pauses
        // shortly AFTER the fade is visually done, never mid-fade.
        let fadeMs = fadeMsOverride != null ? fadeMsOverride : duration * 1.5;
        // CURTAIN-RETURN: hide INSTANTLY, don't animate. A hide fade is an
        // exit animation — it only reads correctly when the visitor watched
        // the particles being there and scrolling away. On a post→close
        // return the page boots fresh at a restored mid-page position where
        // the particles were ALREADY hidden before the visitor left; playing
        // a 600ms fade-out there means the layer paints at full opacity over
        // whatever should be on screen, then dissolves.
        //
        // That is the "gradflow page bg fades in on return" report, measured:
        // the gradflow canvas was fully opaque and correctly coloured the
        // whole time (opacity 1 from t=116ms, stable pixel), while THIS
        // layer ramped 1 -> 0 across t=659..1292ms on top of it. What looked
        // like the background fading in was the particle layer clearing off
        // it. Every earlier fix aimed at the gradflow itself therefore
        // changed nothing.
        //
        // Gated on __curtainReturnLoad, NOT html.curtain-restoring: that
        // class is a veil dropped as soon as the scroll jump settles
        // (~200ms), while this hide fires later (~660ms measured), so the
        // class is already gone by the time it would be checked. The flag is
        // set by the same head guard and survives until the visitor's first
        // real input — see its own comment. Normal scroll-driven hides keep
        // their fade untouched.
        if (window.__curtainReturnLoad) fadeMs = 0;
        const hideDelayMs = fadeMs * (1.75 / 1.5);
        if (layer) { layer.style.transition = `opacity ${fadeMs / 1000}s ease`; layer.style.opacity = '0'; }
        // Pause the render loop only AFTER the fade finishes — pausing
        // immediately would freeze the first hidden frame and the disperse
        // morph above would never be seen.
        clearTimeout(window.__particleHideTimer);
        window.__particleHideTimer = setTimeout(() => {
          // Re-check the layer is ACTUALLY still faded out before pausing the
          // render loop. This timer was armed alongside the fade above, but
          // it fires ~700ms later — and in that window the layer's opacity
          // can have been put back to 1 by another path (the show branch
          // below, or a fresh page's own inline opacity:1 after a curtain
          // return re-runs this file). Pausing the loop then freezes the last
          // drawn frame ON SCREEN at full opacity instead of hiding it.
          //
          // That mismatch is the "particles look stronger / brighter after
          // opening a post and closing it" report: measured on a curtain
          // return, __particleLayerHidden was true while the layer computed
          // opacity 1 — a static, fully-opaque frozen frame sitting where the
          // live animated (and normally fading) object should be. Scrolling
          // away and back fixed it because that fires a fresh trigger, which
          // reaches the show branch and resyncs both.
          const l = document.getElementById('particle-morph-demo');
          const stillFaded = !l || l.style.opacity === '0';
          if (stillFaded) window.__particleLayerHidden = true;
          else window.__particleHiding = false; // fade was cancelled; stay live
        }, hideDelayMs);
        return;
      }
      clearTimeout(window.__particleHideTimer);
      window.__particleLayerHidden = false;
      window.__particleHiding = false;
      // Restore ONLY from a fully-committed hide (opacity exactly '0').
      //
      // A `wasHiding ||` clause was tried here — restore whenever any hide
      // was in flight — and had to be reverted: it made a show call landing
      // mid-fade resurrect the layer unconditionally. That is wrong for a
      // STALE show. Clicking Home and scrolling within ~400ms produces
      // exactly that: the hero entrance is still queued behind its own
      // readiness poll, hero-exit fires and starts fading the layer out, then
      // the late entrance lands mid-fade and yanked opacity back to 1 —
      // leaving the 'terrain' ground plane ("the surface") sitting on screen,
      // which is precisely the bug it was meant to help with.
      //
      // Keeping the strict '0' test means an in-flight fade is left to
      // finish; the hero guard above (scrollY > heroHeight) already refuses
      // stale entrances once you are past the hero, and scroll-scrub-anim.js
      // abandons its queued morph when __particleHiding is set.
      // PRE-PLACE BEFORE REVEALING (2026-08-10). Until this call, the layer
      // went to opacity:1 while the particles were still sitting wherever
      // the PREVIOUS zone had parked them, and only moved once the render
      // loop's next apply() ran — animate() skips apply() entirely while
      // hidden, so nothing had placed them yet. Scrolling up from the footer
      // (which parks the object centred, x:0) into the hero (which offsets
      // it left via its live canvas offset) showed that centred position for
      // a frame or more and then snapped: the reported jump.
      //
      // Ordering matters and is deliberate: __particleLayerHidden was set
      // false just above, which is what lets hero's own heroStillRelevant()
      // gate pass — its frame()/ownsPosition() both return null/false while
      // that flag is true, so calling this any earlier would skip the hero
      // zone entirely and place nothing. Position is written here, THEN the
      // opacity flips below, so the first painted frame is already correct.
      // Shape has its own equivalent (checkShapesEvenWhileHidden) already.
      if (window.particleScrollDirector &&
          typeof window.particleScrollDirector.applyPositionEvenWhileHidden === 'function' &&
          system && system.loop) {
        window.particleScrollDirector.applyPositionEvenWhileHidden(system.loop);
      }
      if (layer && layer.style.opacity === '0') {
        layer.style.transition = 'opacity 0.6s ease';
        layer.style.opacity = '1';
      }
      if (system && typeof system.morphTo === 'function' && target) system.morphTo(target, duration);
    };
  </script>
